--- body ---
## Source
2026-06-04 conversation on migrate vs update. Spotted by code reading
(src/go/migrate.go, migrateLayout), not yet by a field incident --
fix before it becomes one.

## Context
`migrateLayout` calls `installAssets(root, false, false)`:
refuseDiverged=false means files differing from the embedded asset are
overwritten UNCONDITIONALLY. For AGENTS.md that is arguably fine
(agent instructions should track the binary), but `.ergrc` is user
configuration: a project that customized its `[labels]` vocabulary or
`[update] url` loses the edit silently on the next `erg migrate`.

`erg init` already has the right behaviour: refuseDiverged=true runs
the dpkg 3-state compare -- byte-identical: skip; matches the
.erg-assets stamp or a known shipped hash (clean upgrade): overwrite;
matches neither (local edit): preserve, message, exit 2. Only
`init --force` clobbers.

migrate's helpMigrate even advertises the current behaviour
("refreshes init assets (overwrites diverged files without
prompting)") -- so this is documented-but-wrong, not undocumented:
the doc must change with the code.

## Fix
Make migrate's asset refresh use the same protective path as init:
call installAssets with refuseDiverged=true. A preserved local edit
should not fail the migration (migrate's exit code stays governed by
the migration itself); print the same preserve message so the user
knows the file was kept. Update helpMigrate wording accordingly.

Consider in passing whether the migrate summary line should count
skipped assets (it already prints created/refreshed/unchanged).

## Exit criteria
- [ ] Locally-edited .ergrc survives `erg migrate` (test: customize a
      label line, run migrate, byte-compare)
- [ ] Untouched stock .ergrc is still cleanly upgraded by migrate
      (stamp/shipped-hash path still works)
- [ ] helpMigrate no longer says "overwrites diverged files without
      prompting"; describes the 3-state rule instead
- [ ] make check passes